findings.detail:
`apps/api/scripts/prod-migrate.mjs:62-71` (added by this PR):

```
console.log("\n=== post-deploy schema drift check ===");
const drift = spawnSync(process.execPath, [resolve(apiDir, "scripts", "schema-drift.mjs")], {…});
if (drift.status !== 0) {
  console.error("\npost-deploy drift check FAILED");
  process.exit(drift.status ?? 1);
}
console.log("\n✅ Migration applied.");
```

On a drift result the script exits 2 (or 1) and the `✅ Migration applied.` line — previously the only success signal — never prints, even though `run("npx prisma migrate deploy")` completed successfully one step earlier. The script's own header comment (lines 3-12, unchanged) still describes it as: builds the URL, "prints `migrate status`, then runs `migrate deploy`" — it does not mention the drift step at all, so an operator reading the file for the failure's meaning is told the script's last action is the deploy.

Failure scenario: CLAUDE.md's canonical deploy flow makes this script step 1, before the public flip and the merge ("apply the prod migration FIRST … must precede the app deploy"). An operator sees a non-zero exit and `post-deploy drift check FAILED` on a script named `prod-migrate`, concludes the migration did not apply, and aborts the merge / reverts the PR — while production already carries the new schema and the application code that matches it is now NOT being deployed. That is the worst ordering: migrated database, un-deployed app. The reverse mistake is also available: re-running the script is safe (`migrate deploy` is idempotent) but nothing tells the operator that either.

